// GENERATED — do not edit by hand. Run scripts/gen-eu-vacancy-demand.py.
// Sources: Eurostat —
//   • Job vacancy statistics by occupation and NUTS 1 region (OJA breakdowns),
//     annual experimental statistics [jvs_a_isco3_r1]: EMPLOYMENT by ISCO-08
//     occupation × country × year. Each occupation is term-matched to canonical
//     skills (shared skillsTaxonomy terms) and summed per skill, BY COUNTRY
//     (aggregates + NUTS-1 sub-regions dropped).
//   • Job vacancy statistics by NACE Rev. 2 activity, quarterly [jvs_q_nace2],
//     indicator JVR, NACE B-S, unadjusted: each country's own published JOB
//     VACANCY RATE, per quarter.
//
// The occupation release is an employment stock, so on its own it is not
// comparable with the UK/AU/US feeds, which are vacancy counts. Eurostat
// defines the job vacancy rate r as vacancies ÷ (occupied posts + vacancies),
// so vacancies are recovered from an employment base as employment × r / (1 − r)
// — the inversion of that identity, not an adjustment factor. Each country uses
// its OWN rate for the quarter in question (the US wiring does the same with
// the JOLTS state openings rate).
//
// This replaced a flat 2.5% applied to every country in every year. That single
// constant was wrong in both directions at once — Czechia and the Netherlands
// run several times higher than Spain, Greece or Romania — so it overstated
// southern Europe and understated the tight north-western markets.
//
// Annual employment is held flat across its calendar year and carried forward to
// "now"; the rate steps each quarter; months before the first year are zero.

